{{also|Kaffir}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|caffre}}, {{l|en|kaffer}}, {{l|en|kafir}}, {{l|en|kaphar}}, {{l|en|kaphir}}, {{l|en|kafari}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ar|en}} {{m|ar|كَفَّار||infidel}} or {{m|ar|كَافِر||unbeliever}}, both from {{m|ar|كَفَرَ||to cover, to hide}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|offensive}} In Islamic contexts, a non-Muslim. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|historical|offensive}} A member of the [[Nguni]] people of souther Africa, especially a [[Xhosa]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|southern Africa|ethnic slur|now|chiefly|historical}} A [[black]] person. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|historical|offensive}} A language spoken by the [[Nguni]] peoples of southern Africa, especially [[Xhosa]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#*'''1952''', {{w|Doris Lessing}}, ''Martha Quest'', Panther 1974, p. 73:
#*:This man, seeing a white person enter, moved aside for her, but she saw Joss's eyes on her, and said in kitchen '''kaffir''', ‘No, when you've finished.’
{{seeCites}}

